diff --git a/docs/reference/http-api.md b/docs/reference/http-api.md
index 5baf1c0..e5b6c5e 100644
--- a/docs/reference/http-api.md
+++ b/docs/reference/http-api.md
@@ -97,8 +97,31 @@ always send the pinned-target fields and its close-audit paging relies on
 
 Guarded close requests use a request-local compatibility check instead of a
 separate health probe. A request that sends `Idempotency-Key` or `If-Match`
-also sends `retry_protocol: "close-v1"`. Current daemons require the marker;
-older daemons reject the unknown field before the close can run.
+also sends `retry_protocol: "close-v1"` (or `close-v2` with a lease precondition).
+Current daemons require the marker; older daemons reject an unknown field or
+protocol enum before the close can run.
+
+### Exact execution lease close
+
+Send `retry_protocol: "close-v2"` and `If-Lease-Match: <claim_uid>` together.
+The header is mandatory for close-v2; sending it with another protocol returns
+`400 protocol_required`. A close-v1 daemon rejects the unknown close-v2 enum,
+so a strict client cannot silently fall back to an unguarded close. Health or
+capability discovery is not this safety boundary.
+
+The storage close transaction checks local authority, the exact ClaimUID,
+expiry, and the acquire principal's complete holder/instance/client-kind tuple,
+then closes and releases that lease atomically. Standalone and enabled hubs are
+authoritative; spoke cache is not. Without a guard, legacy close behavior is
+unchanged. `If-Match` remains an independent revision precondition.
+
+Caller-authenticated clients use the same actor/holder and optional `client_kind`
+as acquire. Embedding hosts instead derive the principal from their authenticated
+subject using the same resolver as acquire/renew/release; body actor/holder
+strings cannot impersonate an execution. Missing, expired, mismatched tenure,
+and wrong-holder leases return `409 claim_required`, `claim_expired`, `claim_lost`,
+and `claim_denied`, respectively. Strict dry-run is explicitly unsupported: it
+cannot claim to validate a future atomic close.
 
 The field is **optional in the schema** even though current daemons always send
 it. That is deliberate: a version-detection field has to survive version skew,
